A tiny shop that prices an order of a few things.

We start with a helper that turns a number of cents into a dollar string.

def price_str(cents)
  format("$%d.%02d", cents / 100, cents % 100)
end

A product is a name and a price in cents, and it can describe itself.

class Product
  include Comparable

  attr_reader :name, :cents

  def initialize(name, cents)
    @name = name
    @cents = cents
  end

  def to_s
    name + " at " + price_str(cents)
  end

  def <=>(other)
    cents <=> other.cents
  end
end

An order keeps a list of pairs and can total them up.

class Order
  def initialize
    @lines = []
  end

  def add(product, count)
    @lines << [product, count]
    self
  end

  def subtotal
    @lines.reduce(0) do |running, pair|
      running + pair[0].cents * pair[1]
    end
  end

  def count
    @lines.size
  end
end

Now we build a coffee order, work out the tax, and print everything out.

coffee = Product.new("Coffee Beans", 1299)
mug = Product.new("Ceramic Mug", 850)
order = Order.new
order.add(coffee, 2).add(mug, 1)

subtotal = order.subtotal
tax = (subtotal * 8) / 100

puts "items: " + order.count.to_s
puts "subtotal: " + price_str(subtotal)
puts "tax: " + price_str(tax)
puts "total: " + price_str(subtotal + tax)

A short report, using a conditional and a loop.

if subtotal > 3000
  puts "that is a big order"
else
  puts "a modest order"
end

[coffee, mug].sort.each do |product|
  puts product
end
